    // representative photo — replace with a portrait if found
    body: `Bai Suocheng spent decades as one of the most entrenched political figures in Kokang, a Chinese-speaking self-administered zone tucked into Myanmar's Shan State along the Chinese border, serving as chairman of the Kokang Self-Administered Zone and as a lawmaker for the military-aligned Union Solidarity and Development Party. That political standing, and the armed local militia it came with, gave Bai something an ordinary fraud syndicate could never buy on its own: a jurisdiction where a family business could build fortified industrial-scale compounds and run them for more than a decade with total impunity from Myanmar's central government and no meaningful reach for Chinese police across the border.

Inside those compounds — including a sprawling complex prosecutors called Cangsheng Tech Park — the Bai family operation trafficked in people as readily as it trafficked in money. Recruiters used hometown ties and social-media job ads promising high salaries in Thailand or China to lure workers, who were then smuggled across the border, stripped of their documents, and forced under threat of violence to staff round-the-clock telecom and online-investment fraud lines targeting victims back in mainland China; those who resisted or underperformed were reportedly beaten, and Chinese authorities said the network's enforcers were responsible for killing six Chinese nationals inside the compounds. Alongside the forced-labor scam floors, the Bai syndicate ran illegal online gambling operations and collected "protection" payments from other criminal operators in the zone, building a parallel taxation system over Kokang's entire underground economy.

China's Ministry of Public Security had circulated arrest warrants for Bai and his relatives for years, but it took a 2023 military offensive by an ethnic armed alliance inside Myanmar — which overran Kokang's junta-aligned defenses and effectively dismantled Bai's local protection — to finally put him within reach. Myanmar's military government handed Bai Suocheng and his son Bai Yingcang over to Chinese custody in January 2024, and Chinese prosecutors spent the next year and a half building a case that, by the time it reached trial, accused the Bai network of running more than 31,000 individual fraud schemes.

On November 4, 2025, the Shenzhen Intermediate People's Court convicted Bai, Bai Yingcang, and three top associates on charges spanning fraud, intentional homicide, and drug trafficking, finding their operation had defrauded victims of more than 29 billion yuan — roughly $4 billion — and sentencing all five to death; sixteen further defendants tied to the family received lesser prison terms in the same proceeding. Bai never faced execution: he died of illness in custody on November 11, 2025, at age 75, exactly one week after the verdict, while his son and the other co-defendants appealed. Guangdong's provincial high court rejected those appeals and upheld the death sentences on December 24, 2025, and China carried out a wave of executions tied to the broader Kokang scam-family crackdown in the weeks that followed. Bai's case stands as one of the starkest illustrations of what happens when a fraud operation controls not just the call centers that run the scam, but the soldiers, courts, and border checkpoints of the territory the call centers sit on.`,
